Frequency-Localized Dissipation-Moderated Bilinear Estimates for the 3D Navier–Stokes Equations: 

A Conditional Regularity Program in Dyadic Carleson Geometry

Author: Grok XAi, chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek,Ouadi Maakoul

---

Abstract

This version incorporates the main corrections and strategic refinements proposed after Version 4.1. The central conditional theorem remains a dyadic frequency-localized, dissipation-moderated Besov boundedness result, whose proof is complete under two explicitly stated hypotheses. The passage to a genuine regularity criterion is reformulated in a dyadic Carleson framework, where the geometry of frequency localization, spatial localization, and parabolic scaling are naturally compatible. The principal open analytic ingredient is now a dyadic Carleson summation lemma, which is considerably more accessible than the earlier fully localized bilinear estimate. The non-concentration condition is weakened to a maximal-function or measure-theoretic form, and the fully localized dissipation functional is integrated into the conjectures. No unconditional regularity is claimed. The framework is presented as a falsifiable conditional program with clearly identified technical targets and possible weakenings.

---

1. Introduction

1.1. The Navier–Stokes equations

We consider the incompressible Navier–Stokes equations in three dimensions:

\begin{cases}
\partial_t u +(u\cdot\nabla)u=\nu\Delta u-\nabla p,\\[2pt]
\nabla\cdot u=0,\\[2pt]
u(0,x)=u_0(x),
\end{cases}
\tag{1.1}

with normalization \nu=1. The global regularity problem is open.

1.2. Changes from Version 4.1

The following improvements have been made:

1. Explicit constants are tracked in the proof of the conditional Besov boundedness theorem.
2. The low-frequency estimate is refined and made independent of the artificial cut-off j_1.
3. The Carleson program is now formulated in dyadic Carleson geometry, which eliminates the need for the strongest spatial localization lemma.
4. The fully localized dissipation functional D_{j,x} is used concretely in the dyadic Carleson bilinear conjecture.
5. The non-concentration hypothesis is weakened to a maximal-function or measure-theoretic condition.
6. The wavelet and axisymmetric programs are sharpened, with emphasis on the dyadic Carleson characterization.
7. A section on possible weakenings is added, clarifying which parts of the program can be relaxed while retaining a meaningful conditional statement.

