3. Dyadic Bilinear Conjecture and Non-Concentration Hypothesis

3.1. Dyadic frequency-localized dissipation-moderated bilinear conjecture

Conjecture 3.1 (Dyadic bilinear estimate).
There exist absolute constants C>0, \delta>0, \theta>0, and c>0 such that for every divergence-free vector field

u\in L^\infty_{\mathrm{loc}}([0,T);\dot{B}^{-1}_{\infty,\infty})\cap L^2_{\mathrm{loc}}([0,T);\dot H^1),

for every j\ge j_0, every t\in(0,T), and with \tau_j=c2^{-2j}, one has

2^{j(\frac12+\delta)}
\left\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)\right\|_{L^2([t-\tau_j,t];L^2)}
\le
C
(1+\varepsilon_{\tau_j}(t))^{-\theta}
N(t)
\,
\mathcal E_{\tau_j}(t),
\tag{3.1}

where N(t)=\sup_{s\le t}\|u(s)\|_{\dot{B}^{-1}_{\infty,\infty}}.

3.2. Scale-localized dissipation non-concentration hypothesis

Hypothesis 3.2 (Dyadic non-concentration).
Let \delta,\theta be as in Conjecture 3.1 and let C_1 be the constant appearing in Theorem A below. If T^*<\infty is a finite maximal time of existence, then there exist j_1\ge1 and \eta>0 such that

\sup_{t\in[T^*-\eta,T^*)}
\sup_{j\ge j_1}
2^{-j\delta}
(1+\varepsilon_{\tau_j}(t))^{-\theta}
\mathcal E_{\tau_j}(t)
\le
\frac{1}{4C_1}.
\tag{3.2}

This is the same as Hypothesis 3.3 of Version 4.1, retained for the proof of Theorem A.

---

4. Conditional Besov Boundedness Theorem

Theorem A (Conditional Besov boundedness).
Assume Conjecture 3.1 and Hypothesis 3.2 hold. Let u_0\in L^2_\sigma\cap \mathrm{BMO}^{-1}, and let u be the unique local mild solution. If T^*<\infty is a finite maximal time of existence, then there exist \eta>0 and C_0>0 such that

\sup_{t\in[T^*-\eta,T^*)} N(t)
\le
C_0.
\tag{4.1}

Moreover, the same conclusion holds on any compact subinterval of existence; i.e., N(t) is an a priori bounded functional under the two hypotheses.

4.1. Proof of Theorem A

We give the proof with explicit constants.

Fix j_1,\eta as in Hypothesis 3.2. For t\in[T^*-\eta,T^*) and j\ge j_1, set \tau_j=c2^{-2j} and s=t-\tau_j.

From the Duhamel formula and Lemma 7.1,

\|\Delta_j u(t)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
e^{-c_0 c}\|\Delta_j u(s)\|_{L^\infty}
+
C_2 2^j
\int_{t-\tau_j}^t
e^{-c_0 2^{2j}(t-r)}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)(r)\|_{L^\infty}\,dr.
\tag{4.2}

By Bernstein’s inequality,

\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)(r)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
C_3 2^{3j/2}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)(r)\|_{L^2}.
\tag{4.3}

Hence, multiplying by 2^{-j},

2^{-j}\|\Delta_j u(t)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
e^{-c_0 c}N(t)
+
C_2 C_3 2^{3j/2}
\int_{t-\tau_j}^t
e^{-c_0 2^{2j}(t-r)}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)(r)\|_{L^2}\,dr.
\tag{4.4}

Applying Hölder’s inequality in time,

\int_{t-\tau_j}^t
e^{-c_0 2^{2j}(t-r)}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)(r)\|_{L^2}\,dr
\le
\left(
\int_0^{\tau_j} e^{-2c_0 2^{2j}\sigma}\,d\sigma
\right)^{1/2}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)\|_{L^2([t-\tau_j,t];L^2)}.

Since \tau_j=c2^{-2j}, there is an absolute constant C_4 such that

\left(
\int_0^{\tau_j} e^{-2c_0 2^{2j}\sigma}\,d\sigma
\right)^{1/2}
\le
C_4 2^{-j}.

Thus the integral term in (4.4) is bounded by

C_2 C_3 C_4 2^{j/2}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)\|_{L^2([t-\tau_j,t];L^2)}.
\tag{4.5}

Now apply Conjecture 3.1:

2^{j/2}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)\|_{L^2([t-\tau_j,t];L^2)}
\le
C_5 2^{-j\delta}
(1+\varepsilon_{\tau_j}(t))^{-\theta}
N(t)
\mathcal E_{\tau_j}(t).
\tag{4.6}

Substituting into (4.4), we obtain

2^{-j}\|\Delta_j u(t)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
e^{-c_0 c}N(t)
+
C_1 2^{-j\delta}
(1+\varepsilon_{\tau_j}(t))^{-\theta}
N(t)
\mathcal E_{\tau_j}(t),
\tag{4.7}

where C_1=C_2C_3C_4C_5.

Choose c so large that

e^{-c_0 c}\le \frac14.
\tag{4.8}

Then, by Hypothesis 3.2,

2^{-j}\|\Delta_j u(t)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
\frac14 N(t)
+
C_1 \cdot \frac{1}{4C_1} N(t)
=
\frac12 N(t)
\tag{4.9}

for all j\ge j_1.

For the low frequencies j<j_1, we use the elementary bound

2^{-j}\|\Delta_j u(t)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
C_6 2^{j/2}\|u(t)\|_{L^2}
\le
C_6 2^{j_1/2}\|u_0\|_{L^2}.
\tag{4.10}

Define

C_0 = C_6 2^{j_1/2}\|u_0\|_{L^2}.
\tag{4.11}

Then

N(t)
\le
C_0 + \sup_{j\ge j_1} 2^{-j}\|\Delta_j u(t)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
C_0 + \frac12 N(t),
\tag{4.12}

so N(t)\le 2C_0 for all t\in[T^*-\eta,T^*). This proves the theorem. \square

Remark 4.1. The proof shows that the conclusion is independent of the particular singular time T^*: if the hypotheses hold on a compact interval [0,T] of existence, then N(t)\le C(T) for all t\in[0,T].

