5. Dyadic Carleson Bilinear Conjecture and Non-Concentration Hypothesis

For the passage to a genuine \mathrm{BMO}^{-1} regularity criterion, we now work with dyadic Carleson geometry.

5.1. Dyadic Carleson bilinear conjecture

Conjecture 5.1 (Dyadic Carleson frequency-localized bilinear estimate).
There exist absolute constants C>0, \delta>0, \theta>0, and c>0 such that for every divergence-free vector field

u\in L^\infty_{\mathrm{loc}}([0,T);\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy})\cap L^2_{\mathrm{loc}}([0,T);\dot H^1),

for every dyadic cube Q of side length 2^{-j} with j\ge j_0, and every t\in(0,T), one has

2^{j(\frac12+\delta)}
\left(
\int_{Q^*}
|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)(s,y)|^2\,dy\,ds
\right)^{1/2}
\le
C
(1+\varepsilon_Q(t))^{-\theta}
M_{\rm dy}(t)\,
\mathcal E_Q(t),
\tag{5.1}

where Q^*=Q\times(t-\ell(Q)^2,t), \varepsilon_Q(t)=\mathcal E_Q(t)^2, and M_{\rm dy}(t)=\sup_{s\le t}\|u(s)\|_{\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy}}.

Remark 5.2. The right-hand side uses the fully localized dissipation over the same dyadic cylinder Q^*. Thus the functional D_{j,Q}(t) enters naturally: by (2.7), the local dissipation \mathcal E_Q(t) controls the frequency-localized enstrophy on Q^*.

5.2. Maximal-function non-concentration hypothesis

The strong uniform condition (3.2) is too restrictive. We replace it by a maximal-function version, which is closer in spirit to known partial regularity technology.

For j\ge1, define the maximal dissipation coefficient

\mathcal M_{\delta}(t,x)
=
\sup_{\substack{Q\in\mathcal D\\ x\in Q\\ \ell(Q)\le 2^{-j_1}}}
2^{-\delta \log_2(1/\ell(Q))}
(1+\varepsilon_Q(t))^{-\theta}
\mathcal E_Q(t).
\tag{5.2}

Hypothesis 5.3 (Weak maximal non-concentration).
Let \delta,\theta be as in Conjecture 5.1 and let C_1 be the constant in the dyadic Carleson summation lemma below. If T^*<\infty is a finite maximal time of existence, then there exist \eta>0, j_1\ge1, and \alpha\ge0 such that

\left|
\left\{
(x,t)\in\mathbb R^3\times[T^*-\eta,T^*):
\mathcal M_{\delta}(t,x)
>
\frac{1}{4C_1}
\right\}
\right|
\le
\alpha.
\tag{5.3}

Remark 5.4.

· If \alpha=0, this reduces to the strong uniform non-concentration hypothesis.
· If \alpha>0, the exceptional set has small parabolic measure. Combined with the partial regularity theory of Caffarelli–Kohn–Nirenberg, this may be sufficient to rule out blow-up; the present paper does not prove this implication.
· A further weakening replaces the uniform pointwise bound by a weak-L^1 or L^p norm of \mathcal M_\delta. The dyadic Carleson framework is compatible with such measure-theoretic hypotheses.

