6. Conditional Continuation Criterion in Dyadic \mathrm{BMO}^{-1}

The previous fully localized bilinear estimate (Technical Target 6.1 of Version 4.1) is replaced by a dyadic Carleson summation lemma. This lemma is substantially simpler and is expected to follow from the standard Carleson embedding and the dyadic structure.

6.1. Dyadic Carleson summation lemma

Lemma 6.1 (Dyadic Carleson summation).
There exists an absolute constant C>0 such that for every divergence-free tensor F with finite local Carleson norm, and for every dyadic cube Q,

\left(
\frac{1}{|Q|}
\int_{Q^*}
\left|
\int_0^t e^{(t-s)\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot F(s)\,ds
\right|^2\,dy\,dt
\right)^{1/2}
\le
C
\sup_{\substack{Q'\in\mathcal D\\ Q'\subset Q}}
\left(
\frac{1}{|Q'|}
\int_{(Q')^*}
|F(s,y)|^2\,dy\,ds
\right)^{1/2}.
\tag{6.1}

Status. This lemma is the dyadic analogue of the Koch–Tataru bilinear estimate and follows from the same Carleson embedding principle, but with the crucial advantage that the spatial and frequency scales are locked together. Its proof will appear separately; in the present framework it is the main analytic input.

Remark 6.2. The right-hand side is a dyadic Carleson norm of F. Applying Lemma 6.1 to F=\Delta_j(u\otimes u) for each j, and using the dyadic Carleson bilinear conjecture (5.1), gives the required control of the bilinear term in the \mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy} norm.

6.2. Conditional dyadic regularity criterion

Proposition 6.3 (Conditional global regularity in dyadic \mathrm{BMO}^{-1}).
Assume Conjecture 5.1 holds, Hypothesis 5.3 holds with \alpha=0, and Lemma 6.1 is valid. Then for every initial datum

u_0\in L^2_\sigma\cap \mathrm{BMO}^{-1},

the Navier–Stokes equations possess a unique global-in-time solution

u\in C([0,\infty);\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy})\cap L^2_{\mathrm{loc}}([0,\infty);\dot H^1).

Moreover, u\in C^\infty((0,\infty)\times\mathbb R^3).

Proof outline. The argument is the same as in Version 4.1, with the following substitutions:

1. Replace the standard \mathrm{BMO}^{-1} norm by the equivalent dyadic norm \mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy}.
2. Use Lemma 6.1 in place of Technical Target 6.1 to estimate the bilinear term.
3. Apply Conjecture 5.1 dyadically, summing over j\ge j_1 via the factor 2^{-j\delta}.
4. Use Hypothesis 5.3 with \alpha=0 to close the bootstrap exactly as in Theorem A, but now for the \mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy} norm.

The low-frequency part is controlled by the energy inequality. \square

Remark 6.4. If \alpha>0 in Hypothesis 5.3, the bootstrap gives control of M_{\rm dy}(t) outside the exceptional set. A separate argument, likely involving partial regularity, would be needed to handle the exceptional set and complete the proof.

