9. Wavelet Program

The wavelet program is sharpened as follows.

Hypothesis 9.1 (Dyadic Carleson wavelet characterization).
There exists a divergence-free orthonormal wavelet basis \{\psi_\lambda\}_{\lambda\in\Lambda} of L^2_\sigma(\mathbb R^3) such that the dyadic \mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy} norm is characterized by a discrete dyadic Carleson condition:

\|f\|_{\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy}}
\sim
\sup_{Q\in\mathcal D}
\left(
\frac{1}{|Q|}
\sum_{\lambda:\,\mathrm{supp}\,\psi_\lambda\subset Q}
|c_\lambda|^2
\right)^{1/2},
\qquad c_\lambda=\langle f,\psi_\lambda\rangle.
\tag{9.1}

Remark 9.2. This is significantly more plausible than the continuous Carleson characterization of Version 4.1, because the dyadic structure aligns with the wavelet multiresolution. However, even this discrete Carleson condition is a nontrivial open problem.

Program 9.3. Under Hypothesis 9.1, the dyadic Carleson bilinear conjecture (5.1) reduces to a discrete wavelet trilinear estimate. The frequency localization is automatic, and the dissipation moderator may be expressed in terms of the local wavelet coefficients. This provides a concrete route to verifying Conjecture 5.1 in special cases.

