11. Falsifiability and Possible Weakenings

The program remains falsifiable. In addition to the three failure modes identified in Version 4.1, we add the following possible weakenings that would preserve an interesting conditional statement:

1. Weakened bilinear estimate: Replace the factor M_{\rm dy}(t) by N(t) or by a larger norm; this would still yield a Besov boundedness result but not \mathrm{BMO}^{-1} regularity.
2. Measure-theoretic non-concentration: Allow \alpha>0 in Hypothesis 5.3 and use partial regularity to control the exceptional set.
3. Dyadic Carleson norm: If Lemma 6.1 fails in the stated form, a version with an additional logarithmic loss in the scale j may still close with a modified bootstrap.
4. Logarithmic dissipation moderator: Replace (1+\varepsilon_Q)^{-\theta} by (\log(e+\varepsilon_Q^{-1}))^{-\theta}; this weaker factor may be derivable from known energy estimates.

These weakenings show that the framework is robust: even if the strongest form fails, a slightly modified version may still lead to progress.

