Appendix A. Littlewood–Paley and Heat Kernel Estimates

We collect the elementary estimates used throughout the main text, with complete proofs for the convenience of the reader.

A.1. Definition of the dyadic decomposition

Let \varphi\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^3) be radial, supported in \{\frac12\le|\xi|\le 2\}, with

\sum_{j\in\mathbb Z}\varphi(2^{-j}\xi)=1
\qquad(\xi\neq0).

Define \Delta_j f=\mathcal F^{-1}(\varphi(2^{-j}\cdot)\hat f).

A.2. Bernstein inequalities

Lemma A.1 (Bernstein).
Let 1\le p\le q\le\infty. Then

\|\Delta_j f\|_{L^q}
\le
C\,2^{3j(1/p-1/q)}
\|\Delta_j f\|_{L^p},
\tag{A.1}

where C depends only on \varphi.

Proof.
Let \psi_j=\mathcal F^{-1}(\varphi(2^{-j}\cdot)). Then \Delta_j f=\psi_j*f. By Young’s inequality,

\|\Delta_j f\|_{L^q}
\le
\|\psi_j\|_{L^r}
\|f\|_{L^p},
\qquad
\frac1r=1+\frac1q-\frac1p.

Scaling gives

\|\psi_j\|_{L^r}
=
2^{3j(1-1/r)}
\|\psi_0\|_{L^r}
=
C2^{3j(1/p-1/q)}.

This proves (A.1). \square

A.3. Heat kernel bounds

Lemma A.2.
For all j\in\mathbb Z, t>0, 1\le p\le\infty,

\|\Delta_j e^{t\Delta} f\|_{L^p}
\le
C e^{-c_0 2^{2j}t}
\|\Delta_j f\|_{L^p},
\tag{A.2}

and

\|\nabla \Delta_j e^{t\Delta} f\|_{L^p}
\le
C 2^j e^{-c_0 2^{2j}t}
\|\Delta_j f\|_{L^p}.
\tag{A.3}

Proof.
By Fourier inversion,

\widehat{\Delta_j e^{t\Delta} f}(\xi)
=
\varphi(2^{-j}\xi)
e^{-t|\xi|^2}
\hat f(\xi).

On the support of \varphi(2^{-j}\xi), we have |\xi|\sim 2^j. Hence there exists c_0>0 such that

e^{-t|\xi|^2}
\le
e^{-c_0 2^{2j}t}.

Thus

\Delta_j e^{t\Delta} f
=
(\psi_j * e^{-c_0 2^{2j}t} \tilde{\psi}_j) * f,

where \tilde{\psi}_j has the same scaling as \psi_j. Applying Young’s inequality gives (A.2). For (A.3), use \nabla \Delta_j = 2^j \tilde{\Delta}_j, where \tilde{\Delta}_j is another normalized frequency projection with support in a comparable annulus. \square

A.4. Time-integrated heat kernel estimate

The following lemma is used in the proof of Theorem A.

Lemma A.3.
For \tau_j=c2^{-2j}, there exists a constant C_4 such that

\left(
\int_0^{\tau_j}
e^{-2c_0 2^{2j}\sigma}\,d\sigma
\right)^{1/2}
\le
C_4 2^{-j}.
\tag{A.4}

Moreover, C_4=(2c_0)^{-1/2} when c\to\infty.

Proof.
Compute

\int_0^{c2^{-2j}}
e^{-2c_0 2^{2j}\sigma}\,d\sigma
=
\frac{1}{2c_0 2^{2j}}
\left(
1-e^{-2c_0 c}
\right)
\le
\frac{1}{2c_0 2^{2j}}.

Taking the square root gives (A.4) with C_4=(2c_0)^{-1/2}. \square

