Appendix B. Conditional Besov Boundedness Theorem: Complete Proof with Constants

We restate and prove Theorem A with all constants tracked.

B.1. Hypotheses

Conjecture 3.1. There exist C>0,\delta>0,\theta>0,c>0 such that for all j\ge j_0,

2^{j(1/2+\delta)}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)\|_{L^2([t-\tau_j,t];L^2)}
\le
C
(1+\varepsilon_{\tau_j}(t))^{-\theta}
N(t)
\mathcal E_{\tau_j}(t),

where \tau_j=c2^{-2j}.

Hypothesis 3.2. If T^*<\infty, then there exist j_1,\eta>0 such that

\sup_{t\in[T^*-\eta,T^*)}
\sup_{j\ge j_1}
2^{-j\delta}
(1+\varepsilon_{\tau_j}(t))^{-\theta}
\mathcal E_{\tau_j}(t)
\le
\frac{1}{4C_1},

where C_1 is the constant appearing in (B.5).

B.2. Proof

Let t\in[T^*-\eta,T^*), j\ge j_1, s=t-\tau_j. Duhamel:

u(t)=e^{(t-s)\Delta}u(s)
-\int_s^t e^{(t-r)\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot(u\otimes u)(r)\,dr.

Apply \Delta_j:

\Delta_j u(t)
=
\Delta_j e^{\tau_j\Delta}u(s)
-
\int_{t-\tau_j}^t
\Delta_j e^{(t-r)\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot(u\otimes u)(r)\,dr.

Taking L^\infty norms:

\|\Delta_j u(t)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
\|\Delta_j e^{\tau_j\Delta}u(s)\|_{L^\infty}
+
\int_{t-\tau_j}^t
\|\Delta_j e^{(t-r)\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot(u\otimes u)(r)\|_{L^\infty}\,dr.
\tag{B.1}

Linear term. By Lemma A.2 and the definition of N(t),

\|\Delta_j e^{\tau_j\Delta}u(s)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
C e^{-c_0 2^{2j}\tau_j}
\|\Delta_j u(s)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
C e^{-c_0 c}
2^j N(t).

Multiplying by 2^{-j}:

2^{-j}\|\Delta_j e^{\tau_j\Delta}u(s)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
C e^{-c_0 c}N(t).
\tag{B.2}

Bilinear term. For each r\in[s,t],

\|\Delta_j e^{(t-r)\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot(u\otimes u)(r)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
C 2^j e^{-c_0 2^{2j}(t-r)}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)(r)\|_{L^\infty}.
\tag{B.3}

By Bernstein (A.1) with p=2,q=\infty:

\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)(r)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
C 2^{3j/2}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)(r)\|_{L^2}.

Substituting into (B.3) and integrating in time:

\int_s^t
\|\Delta_j e^{(t-r)\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot(u\otimes u)(r)\|_{L^\infty}\,dr
\le
C 2^{5j/2}
\int_s^t
e^{-c_0 2^{2j}(t-r)}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)(r)\|_{L^2}\,dr.
\tag{B.4}

Apply Hölder in time:

\int_s^t
e^{-c_0 2^{2j}(t-r)}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)(r)\|_{L^2}\,dr
\le
\left(
\int_0^{\tau_j}
e^{-2c_0 2^{2j}\sigma}\,d\sigma
\right)^{1/2}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)\|_{L^2([s,t];L^2)}.

By Lemma A.3,

\left(
\int_0^{\tau_j}
e^{-2c_0 2^{2j}\sigma}\,d\sigma
\right)^{1/2}
\le
C_4 2^{-j}.

Therefore the bilinear contribution to 2^{-j}\|\Delta_j u(t)\|_{L^\infty} is bounded by

C_5 2^{5j/2}2^{-j}2^{-j}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)\|_{L^2([s,t];L^2)}
=
C_5 2^{j/2}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)\|_{L^2([s,t];L^2)}.

Now use Conjecture 3.1:

2^{j/2}
\|\Delta_j(u\otimes u)\|_{L^2([s,t];L^2)}
\le
C_6 2^{-j\delta}
(1+\varepsilon_{\tau_j}(t))^{-\theta}
N(t)
\mathcal E_{\tau_j}(t).

Thus

2^{-j}\|\Delta_j u(t)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
C e^{-c_0 c}N(t)
+
C_1 2^{-j\delta}
(1+\varepsilon_{\tau_j}(t))^{-\theta}
N(t)
\mathcal E_{\tau_j}(t),
\tag{B.5}

where C_1=C_5 C_6.

Choose c so large that C e^{-c_0 c}\le \frac14. Then by Hypothesis 3.2,

2^{-j}\|\Delta_j u(t)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
\frac14 N(t)
+
C_1 \cdot \frac{1}{4C_1} N(t)
=
\frac12 N(t)
\qquad(j\ge j_1).
\tag{B.6}

Low frequencies. For j<j_1, by Bernstein and energy inequality,

2^{-j}\|\Delta_j u(t)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
C 2^{-j}2^{3j/2}\|\Delta_j u(t)\|_{L^2}
\le
C 2^{j/2}\|u(t)\|_{L^2}
\le
C 2^{j_1/2}\|u_0\|_{L^2}.
\tag{B.7}

Set

C_0=C 2^{j_1/2}\|u_0\|_{L^2}.

Then

N(t)
\le
C_0+\sup_{j\ge j_1}2^{-j}\|\Delta_j u(t)\|_{L^\infty}
\le
C_0+\frac12 N(t),

so N(t)\le 2C_0. \square

Remark B.1. The constant C_0 depends on j_1,\|u_0\|_{L^2}, and the absolute constants C,C_1. If the hypotheses hold on a compact interval [0,T] rather than only near T^*, the same proof gives N(t)\le C(T) for all t\in[0,T].

