Appendix C. Dyadic Carleson Norm and Equivalence with Continuous \mathrm{BMO}^{-1}

We give a precise statement and proof of the equivalence used in the main text.

C.1. Definitions

For a cube Q\subset\mathbb R^3, let \ell(Q) denote its side length and Q^*=Q\times(0,\ell(Q)^2) the associated parabolic cylinder.

The continuous Koch–Tataru norm is

\|f\|_{\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}}
=
\sup_{Q\subset\mathbb R^3}
\left(
\frac{1}{|Q|}
\int_{Q^*}
|e^{t\Delta}f(y)|^2\,dy\,dt
\right)^{1/2},
\tag{C.1}

where the supremum is over all cubes (or balls) of arbitrary side length and center.

The dyadic variant is

\|f\|_{\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy}}
=
\sup_{Q\in\mathcal D}
\left(
\frac{1}{|Q|}
\int_{Q^*}
|e^{t\Delta}f(y)|^2\,dy\,dt
\right)^{1/2},
\tag{C.2}

where \mathcal D is the family of all dyadic cubes.

C.2. Equivalence

Proposition C.1. There exists an absolute constant C>0 such that for all f\in\mathcal S',

C^{-1}\|f\|_{\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy}}
\le
\|f\|_{\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}}
\le
C\|f\|_{\mathrm{BMO}^{-1}_{\rm dy}}.
\tag{C.3}

Proof.
The first inequality is trivial because dyadic cubes are a subset of all cubes.

For the second inequality, let Q be an arbitrary cube. There exists a dyadic cube Q_0\in\mathcal D such that Q\subset Q_0, \ell(Q_0)\le 2\ell(Q), and Q_0 has comparable center. More precisely, choose the smallest dyadic cube containing Q; its side length is at most twice the side length of Q.

Then

Q^*\subset \widetilde Q_0^*

where \widetilde Q_0 is a cube concentric with Q_0 and side length c\ell(Q_0), with c\le 4. The heat extension e^{t\Delta}f is nonnegative in L^2 norm only in the sense of the L^2 integral; we need a covering argument.

We use the standard fact that the Carleson condition is invariant under fattening of cubes. More precisely, for any cube Q and any constant A\ge1,

\frac{1}{|AQ|}
\int_{(AQ)^*}
|e^{t\Delta}f|^2
\le
C_A
\sup_{Q'\in\mathcal D}
\frac{1}{|Q'|}
\int_{(Q')^*}
|e^{t\Delta}f|^2.

This follows because AQ can be covered by a bounded number of dyadic cubes of comparable size, and the parabolic cylinder (AQ)^* by their associated cylinders.

Applying this with A=4, we obtain

\frac{1}{|Q|}
\int_{Q^*}
|e^{t\Delta}f|^2
\le
C
\sup_{Q'\in\mathcal D}
\frac{1}{|Q'|}
\int_{(Q')^*}
|e^{t\Delta}f|^2.

Taking the supremum over all cubes Q gives the second inequality. \square

Remark C.2. The same equivalence holds with balls replaced by cubes, and with the heat extension replaced by the full Poisson or modified heat extension; the constants depend only on the dimension.

