Appendix D. Dyadic Carleson Summation Lemma

The main new analytic ingredient in Version 5.0 is Lemma 6.1. We give a detailed statement, a proof under a natural weighted Carleson embedding assumption, and a discussion of the remaining open step.

D.1. Statement

Lemma D.1 (Dyadic Carleson summation).
Let F(s,y) be a divergence-free tensor field with finite local Carleson norm. For every dyadic cube Q,

\left(
\frac{1}{|Q|}
\int_{Q^*}
\left|
\int_0^t e^{(t-s)\Delta}
\mathbb P\nabla\cdot F(s)\,ds
\right|^2\,dy\,dt
\right)^{1/2}
\le
C
\sup_{\substack{Q'\in\mathcal D\\ Q'\subset Q}}
\left(
\frac{1}{|Q'|}
\int_{(Q')^*}
|F(s,y)|^2\,dy\,ds
\right)^{1/2}.
\tag{D.1}

D.2. Reduction to a weighted Carleson embedding

We may assume F is supported in Q^*, since the left-hand side only sees the time interval (0,\ell(Q)^2) and the spatial cube Q, but the heat kernel is nonlocal. However, for the dyadic Carleson norm on the right, we may restrict to subcubes Q'\subset Q.

The standard Carleson embedding theorem gives, for any nonnegative measure \mu on \mathbb R^3\times(0,\infty),

\int_{\mathbb R^3\times(0,\infty)}
|e^{t\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot F(s)|^2\,d\mu(s,y)
\le
C
\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}}^2
\int_{\mathbb R^3\times(0,\infty)} d\mu,

where \|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}} is the global Carleson norm of F. To localize, we take \mu to be the restriction of Lebesgue measure to Q^*. Then

\int_{Q^*}
\left|
\int_0^t e^{(t-s)\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot F(s)\,ds
\right|^2\,dy\,dt
\le
C
\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(Q)}^2
|Q^*|,

where \|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(Q)} is the Carleson norm of F restricted to Q^*, i.e.,

\|F\|_{\mathrm{Car}(Q)}
=
\sup_{\substack{Q'\in\mathcal D\\ Q'\subset Q}}
\left(
\frac{1}{|Q'|}
\int_{(Q')^*}
|F(s,y)|^2\,dy\,ds
\right)^{1/2}.

Dividing by |Q| and taking the square root gives (D.1), provided the weighted Carleson embedding holds with constant independent of Q.

Proof of the reduction. The only nontrivial point is that the Carleson embedding theorem for the singular integral operator \mathbb P\nabla\cdot and the heat semigroup holds in the local dyadic setting. This is precisely the content of the dyadic Carleson embedding, which is a standard consequence of the T(1) theorem for Carleson measures and the fact that the kernel of e^{t\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot satisfies the required size and regularity conditions.

D.3. Key estimate

The kernel K_t(x,y) of the operator e^{t\Delta}\mathbb P\nabla\cdot satisfies

|K_t(x,y)|
\le
C t^{-2}
\left(1+\frac{|x-y|^2}{t}\right)^{-2},

and

|\nabla_x K_t(x,y)|
\le
C t^{-5/2}
\left(1+\frac{|x-y|^2}{t}\right)^{-5/2}.

These bounds are sufficient for the Carleson embedding theorem on dyadic cylinders. The proof follows the standard argument using a dyadic Whitney decomposition and the John–Nirenberg inequality; we omit the routine details.

Status. Lemma D.1 is expected to be provable with existing technology. However, we do not claim a complete proof in this manuscript; it remains the central analytic input whose verification would complete the conditional regularity program.

