Appendix E. Maximal Non-Concentration and Relation to Partial Regularity

We clarify the maximal-function form of the non-concentration hypothesis and its relation to the Caffarelli–Kohn–Nirenberg (CKN) theory.

E.1. Maximal dissipation coefficient

For j_1\ge1, define

\mathcal M_\delta(t,x)
=
\sup_{\substack{Q\in\mathcal D\\ x\in Q\\ \ell(Q)\le 2^{-j_1}}}
2^{-\delta \log_2(1/\ell(Q))}
(1+\varepsilon_Q(t))^{-\theta}
\mathcal E_Q(t).
\tag{E.1}

Here \varepsilon_Q(t)=\mathcal E_Q(t)^2 and \mathcal E_Q(t) is the local enstrophy over Q^*=Q\times(t-\ell(Q)^2,t).

E.2. Hypothesis 5.3

Hypothesis E.1 (Weak maximal non-concentration).
If T^*<\infty, then there exist \eta>0, j_1\ge1, and \alpha\ge0 such that

\left|
\left\{
(x,t)\in\mathbb R^3\times[T^*-\eta,T^*):
\mathcal M_\delta(t,x)>\frac{1}{4C_1}
\right\}
\right|
\le
\alpha.
\tag{E.2}

When \alpha=0, this is the strong uniform condition. When \alpha>0, the exceptional set has small parabolic measure.

E.3. Relation to CKN

The CKN theorem states that the singular set \mathcal S has parabolic Hausdorff dimension at most 1. In particular, for any \varepsilon>0, there exists a covering of \mathcal S by parabolic cylinders Q_i^* such that

\sum_i |Q_i|^{3/4-\varepsilon}<\infty.

If the maximal non-concentration condition fails, then for every j_1,\eta, there is a set of positive measure where \mathcal M_\delta>c. This does not directly contradict CKN, because the CKN theorem controls the size of the singular set but not the rate of enstrophy concentration on nonsingular points.

Possible implication. If \alpha is sufficiently small, one may hope to combine the bootstrap outside the exceptional set with a CKN-type argument on the exceptional set to rule out blow-up. This remains open.

