Appendix H. Falsifiability Details and Counterexample Candidates

We provide concrete scenarios where the conjectures could fail, as required for falsifiability.

H.1. Failure of Conjecture 3.1

Consider a vector field u with high-frequency oscillations at scale 2^j concentrated in a small ball of radius 2^{-j}. The bilinear term u\otimes u then has energy at scale 2^j that is comparable to the square of the L^\infty norm, which may be much larger than the right-hand side of (3.1) if the local dissipation is small.

Candidate: A solution of the form

u(t,x)=2^{-j}\psi(2^j x)\sin(2^{2j}t),

where \psi is a compactly supported divergence-free vector field. The frequency is 2^j, the amplitude is 2^{-j}, but the bilinear term may be large if the phase alignment is chosen appropriately.

H.2. Failure of Hypothesis 5.3

Leray’s self-similar blow-up ansatz

u(t,x)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2(T^*-t)}}U\left(\frac{x}{\sqrt{2(T^*-t)}}\right)

predicts enstrophy concentration at rate 2^{2j}, i.e., \mathcal E_Q(t)\sim 2^{-j}, which would give

2^{-j\delta}\mathcal E_Q(t)\sim 2^{-j(1+\delta)}\to0

as j\to\infty, so this does not violate the non-concentration condition. A more aggressive concentration, e.g., \mathcal E_Q(t)\sim 2^{-j(1-\delta)}, would violate it. Whether such concentration can occur in a solution is open.

H.3. Failure of Lemma D.1

If the heat kernel’s nonlocality prevents the Carleson embedding from localizing to a single dyadic cube, Lemma D.1 could fail. A counterexample would be a tensor F supported far from Q but whose heat extension still contributes significantly to Q^*. The heat kernel decays like t^{-3/2}e^{-|x-y|^2/t}, so such far-field contributions are exponentially small but not zero. If the global Carleson norm is large due to far-field activity, the local estimate may fail.

